      "sample_index": 0,
      "text": "The number of ways to select $k$ books from $6$ books such that $k$ is not $0$ and not $6$ is just $\\boxed{5}$.\nTo elaborate further, in a situation such as \"how many ways can I select $k$ books from a pile of 6 identical books with some checked out and others not in the library?\" we can break down the problem.\n\n1. *Authors cannot choose an empty initial list of books or ones that are fully checked out, since even though the books are indistinguishable, a length zero list is still different from a length 6 list*:\n\nThis suggests a lower- to upper-counter establishment method, where the list's attainable subsets are systematically enumerated through its build-in contraindication, namely a wholly empty list and a fully populated one.\n2. We want to select $k$ elements from an $n$-sized set (where $k$ does not equal zero or $n$), with resultant partitions split into portions named $Book_1$, $Book_2$, $Book_3$, $Book_4$, $Book_5$, $Book_6$ - which are all indistinguishable.\n\nSince the possible $k$-size slices do not always need to cover the complete set, as long as the complement of the set doesn't, we can adapt Binomial Combination, which encompasses the successive K formation of subsets within the universal set.\n\n3. Additionally, \n\\begin{align*}\n\\binom{n}{k} k=n\\binom{n-1}{k-1}, \\\\\n\\\nk=0 &\\text{ or } n\n\\end{align*}\n\nThis takes into account all partitions of $\\\\{1,2,3,4,5,6\\\\}$ considering\nwhich sets can't be chosen (a plank or the singleton, which is actually a last and first access respectively not possible).\n\nThe main part of the solution is reducing the value of the fraction to complete the summation.\nWe set $n=5$ and $k=1$,\n\\begin{align*}\n{{6}\\choose{1}} = 6 \\\\\n{{5}\\choose{2}}=10 \\\\\n{{4}\\choose{3}} =4 \\\\\n{{3}\\choose{4}} = 0 \\\\\n{{2}\\choose{5}} = 0 \\\\\n{{1}\\choose{6}} = 0 \\\\\n{{0}\\choose{7}} = 0 \\\\\n\\text{(Not totally unique individually, the above forms}} \\\\\n6\\times 10 \\times 4 = \\boxed{240}\n\\end{align*}\n\nIn general, we create a chain of combinations for the below set: \n$$(n-4)C(r-1)+(n-3- r)C(r) + ... + nC(r)+(n-1-3)C(r-1) + ... + 2C(n+1-r)$$",
