      "sample_index": 3,
      "text": "To find the number of different ways to distribute the six identical books such that at least one book is in the library and at least one is checked out, we can break down the problem as follows:\n\n1. **Identify the Total Number of Distributions**:\n   Each book can either be in the library (L) or checked out. This gives us 2 choices per book. Given 6 books, there are \\(2^6 = 64\\) possible distributions of the books.\n\n2. **Exclude Invalid Distributions**:\n   We need to exclude the two invalid cases where all books are in the library (LLL...L) or all books are checked out (CCCCC...C).\n   \n3. **Calculate Valid Distributions**:\n   We have \\(2^6 - 2 = 64 - 2 = 62\\) valid distributions, as we skip the cases where all books are in the library or all books are checked out, leaving us with 62 different ways.\n\nUsing Python and SymPy, we can confirm this result:\n\n```python\nimport sympy as sp\n\n# Total number of distributions of 6 books (where books are indistinguishable)\ntotal_distributions = 2**6\n\n# Subtract the invalid cases where all books are in the library or all books are checked out\ninvalid_cases = 2\n\n# Valid distributions\nvalid_distributions = total_distributions - invalid_cases\n\n# Output the result\nprint(valid_distributions)\n```\n```output\n62\n```\nThe number of different ways to distribute the six identical books such that at least one book is in the library and at least one is checked out is \\(\\boxed{62}\\).",
